      "data": [
        {
          "id": "8389c230-3c10-3cd4-9d2f-aad0831e4634",
          "content": {
            "id": "8389c230-3c10-3cd4-9d2f-aad0831e4634",
            "contentType": "VIDEO",
            "title": "Novo Nordisk wins US approval for weight-loss pill",
            "description": "<title><body><p>STORY: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration\u00a0approved Novo Nordisk's weight-loss pill on Monday.</p><p>:: Novo Nordisk</p><p>The 25 milligram pill is made of semaglutide, the same active ingredient in injectable Wegovy and Ozempic.</p><p>The pill will be sold under the brand name Wegovy.</p><p>A 64-week, late-stage study showed participants who took 25 mg\u00a0of oral semaglutide once daily lost more body weight on average than those on a placebo.</p><p>The pill was approved for chronic weight management in adults with obesity or overweight and at least one related health condition.</p><p>This expands the patient pool at a time when obesity\u2011related healthcare costs are climbing.</p><p>It could help open the door to tens of millions of untapped patients in a global market, forecast to be worth some $150 billion a year by the next decade.</p><p>The approval could help spur a turnaround for Novo after a rocky year of sliding shares and profit warnings.</p><p>:: Novo Nordisk</p><p>Its injectable Wegovy faces slowing sales amid intense competition from Eli Lilly and pressure from compounded versions.</p><p>Shares of Novo jumped 8% and Lilly fell 1% in extended trading after the approval announcement.</p><p>David Moore, Novo's executive vice president of U.S. operations, said a daily pill could boost interest and uptake of the drug.</p><p>Analysts say the pills could make up about a fifth of the market by 2030.</p></body></title>",
            "summary": "<body><p>STORY: The U.S. Food and Drug Administration&nbsp;approved Novo Nordisk's weight-loss pill on Monday.</p><p>:: Novo Nordisk</p><p>The 25 milligram pill is made of semaglutide, the same active ingredient in injectable Wegovy and Ozempic.</p><p>The pill will be sold under the brand name Wegovy.</p><p>A 64-week, late-stage study showed participants who took 25 mg&nbsp;of oral semaglutide once daily lost more body weight on average than those on a placebo.</p><p>The pill was approved for chronic weight management in adults with obesity or overweight and at least one related health condition.</p><p>This expands the patient pool at a time when obesity\u2011related healthcare costs are climbing.</p><p>It could help open the door to tens of millions of untapped patients in a global market, forecast to be worth some $150 billion a year by the next decade.</p><p>The approval could help spur a turnaround for Novo after a rocky year of sliding shares and profit warnings.</p><p>:: Novo Nordisk</p><p>Its injectable Wegovy faces slowing sales amid intense competition from Eli Lilly and pressure from compounded versions.</p><p>Shares of Novo jumped 8% and Lilly fell 1% in extended trading after the approval announcement.</p><p>David Moore, Novo's executive vice president of U.S. operations, said a daily pill could boost interest and uptake of the drug.</p><p>Analysts say the pills could make up about a fifth of the market by 2030.</p></body>",
